.TH KRYLOVTEST 1
.SH NAME
krylovtest \- test the Krylov solvers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B krylovtest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Krylovtest
solves systems whose answers are known in advance with both of
.IR krylov (2)'s
methods, and checks the number of iterations each takes as well as the
residual it reaches.
.PP
The iteration count is the point.
Both solvers build a basis and depend on it staying orthogonal, and the
vector updates that maintain it happen in place through
.IR math (2)'s
.BR axpby ,
so a mistake there damages a basis vector rather than crashing.
That does not produce a wrong answer: GMRES with a damaged basis
usually still converges, only slower.
A residual check alone would pass.
The iteration count is what moves.
.PP
The systems are diagonal with a chosen number of distinct eigenvalues,
because a Krylov method converges in at most that many iterations and
for these matrices takes exactly that many \(em the sharpest statement
available about whether a basis is doing its job.
.PP
One case is deliberately non-symmetric, where conjugate gradients has no
business converging and GMRES must.
Without it the whole test could pass on symmetric problems alone, and
the Arnoldi recurrence \(em the part that actually keeps a stored basis
\(em would never be exercised on anything CG could not have done.
A second runs GMRES with a restart shorter than the iterations needed,
so the basis is rebuilt rather than grown once.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/krylovtest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR krylov (2),
.IR math-linalg (2),
.IR vecbench (1)
.SH BUGS
It does not catch an
.B Apply
that returns a reused buffer rather than a fresh array.
That was tried: every count and residual here is unchanged by it,
because the basis vector GMRES keeps is a copy rather than the array
.B Apply
handed back.
The solver does modify that array, so reusing one is still unwise, but
that is a caution in
.IR krylov (2)
and not something this test can detect.
.PP
Dense matrices only, and small ones.
Nothing here exercises
.IR sparse (2),
preconditioning \(em there is none \(em or a matrix badly enough
conditioned to make either method struggle.
